  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe cold front was able to pass through with just a few showers in \nthe northern Thumb and gusty westerly winds across the rest of the \narea. Subsidence from high pressure building into the region in the \nwake of the front this evening will quickly scour out the remaining \ncloud field and allow for a cool night with lows dropping into the \n50s for most locations. \n\nMain longwave trough flattens a bit tonight into tomorrow as heights \nattempt to rise slightly from the south. Surface high and shortwave \nridge will be over the region the first half of Sunday keeping the \narea dry, but chances of showers and thunderstorms begin to increase \nonce again in the late afternoon and evening as upstream convection \nattempts to drift into SE MI north of a developing warm front. A \nsurface low will deepen over the Plains which then turns the stalled \ncold front draped along the Ohio Valley into a warm front and draws \nit back northward. Strong low level jet up through Iowa tonight will \nattempt to fire off a round of convection that will track along the \nelevated warm front across Lake MI and into SW lower MI early Sunday \nbefore potentially running out of instability and scouring out. \nChance pops will begin west of I75 Sunday evening to account for the \npotential eastward shift of the convective complex. SPC Day 2 \noutlook highlights southern MI along the border with a Marginal Risk \nof severe weather for the potential for this, and or any other \nconvection, riding along the warm front. \n\nAdditional storm chances Sunday night into Monday as the trough \npushes the system eastward resulting in the low level jet \nintersecting the warm front near Chicago. This round of convection \nis then expected to track along the front and near the state line \nand into SE MI early Monday morning. SPC Day 3 outlook has an area \nof Slight Risk up through Detroit with Marginal Risk extending \nfurther north to Saginaw. Very moist airmass with surface dewpoints \nnear 70F and PWATs in excess of 1.75 inches will lead to very \nefficient rainfall with the storms so in addition to severe weather \nthreats, heavy rainfall will be a threat as well.  Especially if \nmultiple round of storms pass through the area through the day.\n\nWe remain in the active northwest flow pattern through midweek with \npotential for periodic rain chances. Slightly cooler pattern \nexpected late in the week with highs only topping out in the upper \n70s.",
